\section{What it reads}{

It reads \code{SLURM_JOB_ID} first. It falls back to \code{SLURM_JOBID}, which the
\code{sbatch} manual page keeps for backward compatibility. Measured on Slurm
25.11.2: a login shell carries no \code{SLURM_} variable at all, and a one-task
job carries 33 of them.

Any non-empty value counts as inside a job. A digit check would be wrong,
because a heterogeneous job carries an id such as \code{17+1}. A false negative
there makes the payload submit itself, which costs more than a false
positive from a hand-set variable.

\code{plnr::is_run_directly()} does not answer this question. It tests the call
depth, not the environment. Measured on this machine, it returns \code{TRUE} on
the login node and inside a Slurm job.
}

